        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "I want to say something simple about democracy.\n\nToo many people think democracy is something distant—something written in a constitution, built into a political system, or discussed as an abstract ideal.\n\nBut democracy is also a habit of mind.\n\nYou do not have to say the word “democracy” every day. The real test comes when your neighbor is suddenly taken away, or your coworker is fired for no good reason.\n\nIf your first reaction is, “It has nothing to do with me,” “I should distance myself before I get involved,” or “They must have done something wrong,” then something has already failed.\n\nOrdinary people are often the ones with the least power. That is exactly why our attitude toward one another matters.\n\nWhen a coworker is fired, do you celebrate because it was not you—or do you ask why?\n\nAnd if they did nothing wrong, but were punished simply for demanding fair pay, reasonable time off, or better working conditions, do you stay silent, or do you stand up for them?\n\nTo me, that choice is one of the clearest expressions of democratic consciousness.\n\nDemocracy begins when people care about injustice even when it has not reached them yet."
        },
